# p_7c3e — Mara works part-time (9:00–13:00); the afternoon is for gym and errands (fork of p_b4a2)

Mara works a part-time job 9:00–13:00 on weekdays (a nearby office or clinic). She takes her keys, wallet, backpack, jacket, phone, lunchbox, laptop, charger, notebook, glasses, and headphones with her to work. In the afternoon (13:00–17:00) she goes to the gym or runs errands; the house is empty. She is home by 17:00 for dinner, evening yoga, and couch time with the tablet and remote. The tablet stays at the kitchen counter when she leaves in the morning — she does not carry it to work (the employer provides its own tech) and does not take it to the gym. Her tech kit (laptop, charger, notebook, glasses, headphones) lives at the kitchen table in the morning and evening; she packs it into her backpack before leaving.

What changed from the parent (p_b4a2): (1) The tablet is now at counter_k1 during weekday 9–17 h, not desk_b1. The parent's tablet-at-desk claim went against 6 times; the tablet's most common receptacle is counter_k1 (6/12 sighted days). (2) The laptop, charger, notebook, glasses, and headphones are now at kitchen_table_k1 as their base location (matching 5–7/8–10 sighted days each) and OUT_OF_HOUSE during weekday 9–17 h (she takes them to work). The parent had them at desk_b1, which contradicts the sighting data. (3) The water bottle base is now nightstand_b1 (3/10 sighted days there, 6 receptacles total — very mobile) with OUT_OF_HOUSE during 9–17, rather than counter_k1. (4) The phone block is retained at nightstand_b1 base with OUT_OF_HOUSE 9–17.

What would refute this fork: the tablet found at desk_b1 on a weekday at 15:00 (she is at the gym; the tablet is at the counter), the laptop found at kitchen_table_k1 on a weekday at 11:00 (she took it to work), or the keys found at entry_table_e1 on a weekday at 10:00.

