# p_2a9f — Mara works from home; a noon walk takes the lunchbox out the door (fork of p_6f9d)

Mara works from home at the kitchen table (9:00–17:00 weekdays) but takes a 30-minute walk at 12:30 each weekday, bringing the lunchbox to eat on a bench. She is back by 13:00, washes the lunchbox, and it dries in the dish rack. The tablet is her reference device and sits at the kitchen counter during the work day (she glances at it but works on the laptop at the kitchen table); the coffee table is for evenings and weekends. Her keys, wallet, and backpack stay at the entry in the morning and evening but are in her pocket during the work day (ON_PERSON), which is why the robot's surface-level looks at the entry come up empty during 9–17 h. In the morning she cooks at the counter (pan on the counter, not the table) and the pan goes back to the sink after.

What changed from the parent (p_6f9d): (1) The pan is now at counter_k1 during the 7–10 h cooking window, not kitchen_table_k1. The parent's pan-at-kitchen-table claim went against 6 times total (4 before this call, 2 since). The pan's most common receptacle is kitchen_table_k1 (6/12) but it is NOT there during the morning cooking window — it is at the counter while in use. (2) The tablet is now at counter_k1 during weekday 9–17 h, not desk_b1. The parent's tablet-at-desk claim went against 9 times; the tablet's most common receptacle is counter_k1 (6/12 sighted days). (3) The laptop, charger, notebook, glasses, and headphones are now at kitchen_table_k1 as their base (matching the sighting data) rather than desk_b1.

What would refute this fork: the pan found at kitchen_table_k1 on a weekday at 8:00 (she is cooking at the counter), the tablet found at desk_b1 on a weekday at 11:00 (it is at the counter), or the lunchbox found at the counter at 12:30 on a weekday (she is out walking).

